# Environment Variables

# ── LLM PROVIDER ROUTING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every LLM action runs on Gemini; the gateway picks the model by tier (lib/handlers/gemini.js):
#   heavy     gemini-3.8-flash       buildContext, mergeContext, analyzeSignals, extractDocumentIntel, answerQuestion
#   light     gemini-3.5-flash-lite  generateQueries, frameDecision
#   reasoning gemini-3.1-pro-preview generateSummary, generateDecisionBrief, interpretShifts
#   Sarvam          = cheap cross-provider FALLBACK only, when Gemini itself is unavailable
#   Claude/Groq     = fully wired but OFF (enable via the VITE_LLM_* vars below when ready)
#   Jev (TypeSafe)  = typed decisions (scores / yes-no), not text — see the Jev section below
#
# Provider selection (optional — defaults shown):
# VITE_LLM_PRIMARY=gemini      # heavy + light actions
# VITE_LLM_REASONING=gemini    # the three judgment/synthesis actions; defaults to PRIMARY
# VITE_LLM_FALLBACK=sarvam     # single cross-provider fallback
# VITE_CONTEXT_LLM_PROVIDER=gemini            # override buildContext provider only
# VITE_CONTEXT_ESCALATION_PROVIDER=none       # weak-context escalation ('none' disables; set to 'claude' later)
# To enable Claude later: set the relevant VITE_LLM_* var(s) above to 'claude' and provide CLAUDE_API_KEY.
#
# Credibility harness (opt-in, default OFF). When 'true', every web signal must carry a
# verbatim evidence_quote found in the analyzed source — signals without one are dropped
# (the quote-or-drop rule doc-intel uses for uploaded files, extended to web signals).
# Leave off until you can watch a run; turning it on makes signal analysis stricter.
# VITE_SIGNAL_QUOTE_BINDING=false

# Gemini API (Required — every LLM action + document embeddings)
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_gemini_api_key_here
# Optional: override the LEAD model of a tier (the rest of the tier's list stays as fallback).
# heavy:     gemini-3.8-flash → gemini-3.6-flash → gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.8-flash
# light:     gemini-3.5-flash-lite → gemini-3.8-flash
# GEMINI_LIGHT_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# reasoning: gemini-3.1-pro-preview (preview-only) → gemini-3.8-flash → gemini-3.6-flash
# GEMINI_REASONING_MODEL=gemini-3.1-pro-preview
# Every call reports its model, thinking tokens, finish reason and any fallback used in the
# run's provenance (x-llm-model / x-llm-thoughts / x-llm-finish / x-llm-fallbacks).
# Test-only: point the gateway at a fake Gemini API.
# GEMINI_API_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta

# Sarvam API (cross-provider fallback)
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint. If absent, its actions fall back to Gemini.
# SARVAM_API_KEY=your_sarvam_api_key_here
# SARVAM_MODEL=sarvam-m                                   # optional override
# SARVAM_API_URL=https://api.sarvam.ai/v1/chat/completions  # optional override
# SARVAM_JSON_MODE=false   # set 'true' only if your Sarvam deployment supports response_format:json_object

# Jev (TypeSafe) — typed decisions (scores, yes/no), not text. SERVER-SIDE ONLY: never prefix
# with VITE_ (that inlines it into the browser bundle). /api/jev accepts only the pipeline's
# internal key, so no signed-in user can spend the quota. Without a key every Jev use case
# falls back to its rule-based behaviour.
# JEV_API_KEY=your_typesafe_api_key_here
# JEV_MODEL=jev-latest
# JEV_API_URL=https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone
# JEV_CONCURRENCY=8          # lower it if your TypeSafe plan's rate limit is tighter
# Mode per use case: off | shadow | on. Default shadow = recorded on the report, changes nothing.
# JEV_MODE=shadow            # default for every use case below
# JEV_DEEP_READS=shadow      # rank which search results get a full-page read
# JEV_TRIAGE=shadow          # when the observation cap binds, keep the most material ones
# JEV_WATCH=shadow           # suppress cosmetic changes to tracked pages
# JEV_SYNTHESIS=shadow       # confidence on shifts / blind spots / opportunities
# JEV_NEWS=shadow            # rank "News that matters" by relevance to the company
# JEV_REVIEW=shadow          # advisory brief checks (warnings only, never blocking)

# Claude API (wired but OFF by default — enable via VITE_LLM_* above)
# CLAUDE_API_KEY=your_claude_api_key_here
# CLAUDE_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6   # optional override

# Groq API (Optional — no longer in the default routing; kept as an available gateway)
# GROQ_API_KEY=your_groq_api_key_here
# GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile

# Optional: prompt size controls for quota/cost tuning (server-side)
# PROMPT_CONTEXT_CHAR_LIMIT=60000
# PROMPT_SIGNALS_CHAR_LIMIT=120000
# PROMPT_SUMMARY_CHAR_LIMIT=50000

# Optional: full-pipeline time budget in ms. Default 45000 is Hobby-safe (60s function cap).
# On Vercel Pro (worker maxDuration 300 in vercel.json) set this to 270000.
# PIPELINE_MAX_MS=45000

# ── WEB DATA / SCRAPING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Scraping (api/scrape.js): Parallel AI Extract (primary) → Exa (fallback)
PARALLEL_AI_API_KEY=your_parallel_ai_api_key_here
# Exa (Required — scrape fallback + neural search / competitor validation in api/exa.js)
EXA_API_KEY=your_exa_api_key_here

# Tavily API (Required — primary for deep research search; comma-separated for dual-key rotation)
TAVILY_API_KEY=your_tavily_api_key_here
# TAVILY_API_KEY=key1,key2   # dual key: rotates to key2 on 429

# Supabase (Required for Auth)
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=your_supabase_project_url_here
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_supabase_anon_key_here

# Supabase Admin (Required for server-side admin user management)
SUPABASE_URL=your_supabase_project_url_here
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your_service_role_key_here

# Dedicated secret for internal server-to-server calls (the intelligence job runner).
# Set this in prod so the service-role key isn't reused as a shared header secret.
# If unset, the code falls back to SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY (transitional only).
# INTERNAL_API_KEY=generate_a_long_random_string_here

# Sign-in — exactly ONE email address may sign in, instantly (no password, no email
# click). Every other address is refused. No admin role, no self-serve signup.
# The account joins the client whose domain matches it (sql/seed_bahirjee_in_domain.sql).
# ⚠️  No ownership proof: anyone who types this address gets in and can start runs.
# LOGIN_EMAIL=admin@bahirjee.in

# Brief attestation mode — 'report' (default), 'enforce', or 'off'.
# Checks the finished brief against its own evidence: claims trace to a source, confidence
# is within what the evidence supports, the read names what would overturn it, a shallow
# run discloses its gaps, and no instructional language crept in.
# Defaults to 'report' rather than 'enforce' because the only enforcing action available
# would be withholding a brief the founder already paid a full run for. Measure first.
# BRIEF_POLICY_MODE=report

# Upgrade destination. Set to a Stripe Payment Link (or any checkout URL) and the
# "Upgrade to Premium" action goes straight there. Unset falls back to a mailto to
# VITE_UPGRADE_CONTACT_EMAIL, which is fine at beta volume.
# VITE_UPGRADE_URL=https://buy.stripe.com/...

# Production URL — optional override. Must NOT be localhost or it will be ignored.
# Leave unset in Vercel; VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL is auto-injected and used automatically.
# SITE_URL=https://bahirjee-poc.vercel.app

# Optional: Restrict API CORS by origin list (comma-separated)
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://bahirjee-poc.vercel.app,http://localhost:5173

# Optional: Global API rate limiting (per-IP, per-endpoint)
# API_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=60
# API_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000

# Optional: Custom API Base URL (for local testing)
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Optional: pre-trim raw website context before sending to backend (client-side)
# VITE_CONTEXT_INPUT_CHAR_LIMIT=60000

# ── Agent runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Overlay that names which agent owns the step currently running, records agent runs,
# artifacts and handoffs, and enforces per-agent budgets.
#   resolve — step order comes from the agent registry (DEFAULT)
#   observe — record agent metadata, but source step order from the pipeline
#   off     — no agent metadata written at all
# `resolve` is safe as the default because the agent-runtime test asserted the registry's step
# order is identical to nextPipelineStep()'s. Both fallbacks are env-only, so reverting the
# agent layer never needs a deploy.
# AGENT_RUNTIME_MODE=resolve

# Resume a job whose checkpoint was written by a DIFFERENT graph version. Off by default:
# a reordered step list resolves every step name successfully and then silently skips or
# repeats work, so a mismatched checkpoint is failed and re-queued rather than resumed.
# Set to 1 only when you know the graph bump was cosmetic.
# ALLOW_GRAPH_VERSION_MISMATCH=1

# Tool-grant enforcement. Each agent spec declares `allowed_tools`; this decides what happens
# when a step calls a provider its agent did not declare.
#   strict — fail the step (DEFAULT). A tool grant is an authorization boundary.
#   warn   — log the violation and allow the call. Use while grants are still being trusted.
#   off    — do not wrap the transport at all.
# Only provider routes (gemini/claude/groq/sarvam/search/scrape/exa) are enforceable; Supabase
# access does not pass through the guarded transport and remains declaration-only.
# AGENT_TOOL_ENFORCEMENT=strict

# Verifier gate. Runs before the report reaches the database.
#   enforce — DROP ungrounded and duplicate signals (DEFAULT). Measured over 49 real reports
#             / 997 signals: 3.8% dropped, all ungrounded. Two reports emptied entirely, both
#             holding only unsupported claims — stepPersist records a degradation so a thin
#             report says why it is thin.
#   report  — compute the verdict and record what WOULD be dropped, but persist everything.
#             Use to measure a threshold change before adopting it. Was the old default, which
#             meant the gate never actually gated.
#   off     — skip the gate entirely.
# VERIFIER_MODE=enforce

# Report replay idempotency (v18_reports_run_id.sql). No env var — the column is detected at
# runtime. Without the migration, saveReport() warns once and falls back to a plain insert:
# the report is still saved, but a replayed `persist` step will create a duplicate row.

# CORS origin allowlist (comma-separated). The deployment ALSO trusts its own origin
# automatically, derived from VERCEL_URL / VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL / SITE_URL — Vercel
# mints a new hostname per branch deployment, so previews can never be listed here by hand.
# That is same-origin by construction and does not widen the policy; set this only to allow
# ADDITIONAL origins.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://bahirjee-poc.vercel.app,http://localhost:5173

# ── Track 2: FalkorDB memory & coordination graph ───────────────────────────
# One named graph per tenant (bahirjee:{client_id}); the name is derived
# server-side in lib/_graph.js, never from a request. Server-side only —
# never prefix these with VITE_.
#
# Local Docker:   docker run -p 6379:6379 -it --rm falkordb/falkordb
#                 → FALKORDB_URL=falkor://localhost:6379 (default, no auth)
# FalkorDB Cloud: console at https://app.falkordb.cloud — host/port + password
#                 come from the instance page. The FREE tier has NO TLS, so its
#                 URL is plain falkor://; paid tiers enable TLS via falkors://.
#                 Note: free instances stop after 1 idle day / delete after 7.
# FALKORDB_URL=falkor://localhost:6379
# FALKORDB_URL=falkor://YOUR-HOST.r1.falkordb.io:PORT    # cloud free tier
# FALKORDB_URL=falkors://YOUR-HOST.r1.falkordb.io:PORT   # cloud paid (TLS)
# FALKORDB_USERNAME=
# FALKORDB_PASSWORD=
#
# '1' turns the memory graph on. Anything else (including unset) = degraded
# mode: writes no-op, reads return graph_unavailable, the UI labels answers
# accordingly. This flag IS the graph-removal acceptance test.
# MEMORY_GRAPH_ENABLED=0
#
# Hard ceiling on Reason→Tool→Observe steps per question (default 4, max 8).
# MEMORY_GRAPH_MAX_STEPS=4
#
# Most time (ms) a completed analysis run may spend writing itself into the graph
# (Company/Entities/Session/Findings). Past it, writes stop; the run is unaffected.
# GRAPH_RUN_WRITE_BUDGET_MS=8000

# Optional: per-request timeout for every Supabase call (ms, default 20000).
# fetch has no default timeout, so a stalled PostgREST request otherwise hangs an
# invocation forever — locally there is no function cap to kill it. Keep this below
# STEP_MAX_MS (45000) so a hung call surfaces as a step error with budget left to fail
# cleanly. See src/lib/fetchWithTimeout.js.
# SUPABASE_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# ── Structured source connectors (P3) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Sources that keyword search cannot reach: live tenders, regulatory filings, and the
# engagement metadata search indexes strip off social threads.
#
# SHIPS DARK. Nothing runs until you name it here. Enable one at a time and watch a
# real run before adding the next — see the internal design log.
#   VITE_CONNECTORS_ENABLED=hn,sec_edgar,contracts_finder
#   VITE_CONNECTORS_ENABLED=all
# VITE_CONNECTORS_ENABLED=

# Keyless, verified working 2026-08-03: hn, sec_edgar, contracts_finder, gem
# (gem is a best-effort scrape of a page with no public API and will break without warning)

# US federal contract opportunities. Free key: https://sam.gov/content/api-keys
# UNVERIFIED — the gateway 404s unauthenticated, so neither path nor payload could be
# confirmed without a key. Treat the first real run as the verification.
# SAM_GOV_API_KEY=

# UK company filings — the only route to a PRIVATE UK competitor's real numbers.
# Free key: https://developer.company-information.service.gov.uk/
# COMPANIES_HOUSE_API_KEY=

# Reddit now 403s every unauthenticated JSON endpoint (verified 2026-08-03), so it needs
# an OAuth app. Free: https://www.reddit.com/prefs/apps (type "script")
# REDDIT_CLIENT_ID=
# REDDIT_CLIENT_SECRET=

# SEC and Reddit block generic User-Agents. Set a real contact address before enabling.
# CONNECTOR_USER_AGENT=Bahirjee Intelligence (contact: you@yourdomain.com)

# Vercel Deployment Protection bypass (PREVIEW deployments).
# Auto-injected by Vercel once "Protection Bypass for Automation" is enabled on the project.
# Without it, preview deployments answer the worker self-trigger with an auth challenge:
# the job is never claimed, sits `pending`, and the 2-minute reaper fails it with
# "Analysis could not start". Production and local are unaffected. Nothing to set by hand.
VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE MONDAY LETTER — outbound mail
#
# Until these are set, nothing is emailed. lib/_mail.js self-discloses once per process
# and returns { sent: false, reason: 'no_provider' }; the run still completes and the
# brief is still readable in the app. A missing key is never silent, and never fatal.
#
# Resend API key: https://resend.com/api-keys
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From: address. Must be on a domain verified with the provider, or every send is
# rejected. Bahirjee writes in the first person, so this should read like a person:
#   MAIL_FROM=Bahirjee <bahirjee@yourdomain.com>
# MAIL_FROM=

# Where replies go. Optional — but a letter nobody can reply to is a notification, and
# the early replies are the most useful product feedback available.
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# '0' stops all outbound letters without removing the keys. Use it to pause delivery in
# an environment that is otherwise fully configured (staging, a restore test, a backfill
# that would otherwise re-send weeks of letters). Anything else — including unset — means
# letters are on.
# LETTER_ENABLED=

# Where the links in a letter point. Already used for magic-link redirects; now also the
# base for the letter's "See the evidence" and unsubscribe URLs, resolved in
# lib/_siteUrl.js. If it cannot be resolved the letter ships WITHOUT those buttons rather
# than with a localhost link — see absoluteUrl(). Vercel injects
# VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL / VERCEL_URL automatically; set SITE_URL only to override.
# SITE_URL=

# ⚠️  Requires scripts/migrations/v33_letter_opt_out.sql. Without it, opt-outs cannot be
#     honoured — delivery falls back to writing to every active member and logs loudly.

# "Is this you?" — the five-sentence confirmation screen that replaces the full context
# field editor. '1' turns it on. Off by default so it can be enabled per environment for
# beta clients and rolled back in one deploy; the full editor stays reachable from it via
# "Edit every detail", and is used automatically when too little context resolved to make
# five sentences worth showing. Both screens record corrections identically.
# VITE_CONTEXT_CONFIRM_V2=1